  {
   "id": "04_C4_C4b_C4c-002",
   "finding": "report polish (3258 vs 3423: physical reason for C4)",
   "old": "which rises with wavelength because less of the light reaches and harms the retina:",
   "new": "which rises with wavelength because the retina's pigment absorbs less of the light that reaches it (§C4c subsection 1):",
   "why": "Give the same physical reason for C4 as §C4c."
  },
  {
   "id": "04_C4_C4b_C4c-003",
   "finding": "report #1 (remaining part: rounding), #9, #14 (3265), #25, #26, #27",
   "old": "- **905 vs 940 nm.** Table 9 of the standard gives $C_4$ as in Eq. (63) over 700–1,050 nm, so $C_4(905)=10^{0.002\\times205}=10^{0.41}=2.5704$ and $C_4(940)=10^{0.002\\times240}=10^{0.48}=3.0200$. Their ratio is $10^{0.002\\times(940-905)}=10^{0.07}=1.1749$: a **17.49% increase** for any term containing $C_4$ [E]. Every Class 1 limit this report uses between 700 and 1,050 nm (single pulse, pulse train via $C_5$, and long-exposure average power; table in §C4c) contains $C_4$, so a 940 nm design may emit 17.5% more than an otherwise identical 905 nm design whichever rule binds. This is a gain in permitted emission, not in range or signal-to-noise ratio [E].\n- **905 vs 1,550 nm, long exposure.** A small source is one whose apparent angular size seen from the eye is $\\alpha\\le1.5$ mrad (§C4c); it is the most restrictive case. For a small source, Table 3 gives $3.9\\times10^{-4}C_4C_7$ W at 905 nm, where $C_7$ is a further correction factor that equals 1 at this wavelength ($C_7=1$). That is about **1.00 mW** ($3.9\\times10^{-4}\\times2.5704=1.002\\times10^{-3}$ W), against about **10 mW** at 1,550 nm (corresponding long-exposure entry) [P/E]. Adjusted for photon energy, the tabular ratio is $(10/1.002)(1550/905)=9.98\\times1.713\\approx17.1$: a joule carries $1/E_{\\rm ph}=\\lambda/hc$ photons, where $E_{\\rm ph}$ is the photon energy, $h$ Planck's constant and $c$ the speed of light, so a joule at 1,550 nm holds $1550/905$ times as many photons as at 905 nm. That explains the scale of Luminar's \"17×\" claim, **but the prescribed measurement apertures differ**: 7 mm for the retinal band (wavelengths the eye focuses onto the retina; 7 mm is roughly a dilated pupil), 3.5 mm for long 1,550 nm exposures. The ratio is not a matched transmitter-output or real-scene photon-flux comparison, nor a verified sensor-level range or compliance advantage.\n- **Pulses: the edition matters.** The $2\\times10^{-7}C_4$ J near-IR single-pulse expression is from the **2007** edition, so pulse limits of 0.514/0.604 µJ (905/940 nm) derived from it ($2\\times10^{-7}\\times2.5704=0.514$ µJ and $2\\times10^{-7}\\times3.0200=0.604$ µJ) should not be presented as IEC 60825-1:2014 results. The 2014 edition changed the pulsed Class 1 accessible emission limits (AELs): **198/233 nJ** per isolated pulse for a small source, falling to **79/93 nJ** for long pulse trains (§C4c), because the per-pulse limit is reduced when many pulses can enter the eye within the standard's reference time. In each pair the 940 nm value is the 905 nm one times $C_4(940)/C_4(905)=1.1749$: $198\\times1.1749\\approx233$ and $79\\times1.1749\\approx93$. A 1,550 nm entry such as 8 mJ has its own pulse-duration conditions, so dividing it by the 2007 near-IR entry is not a valid general comparison.",
   "new": "- **905 vs 940 nm.** Table 9 of the standard gives $C_4$ as in Eq. (63) over 700–1,050 nm, so $C_4(905)=10^{0.002\\times205}=10^{0.41}=2.570$ and $C_4(940)=10^{0.002\\times240}=10^{0.48}=3.020$. Their ratio is $10^{0.002\\times(940-905)}=10^{0.07}=1.175$, a **17.5% increase** in any term containing $C_4$ [E]. Every Class 1 limit this report uses between 700 and 1,050 nm (single pulse, pulse train via $C_5$, and long-exposure average power; table in §C4c) contains $C_4$, so a 940 nm design may emit 17.5% more than an otherwise identical 905 nm design whichever rule binds. This is a gain in permitted emission, not in range or signal-to-noise ratio [E].\n- **905 vs 1,550 nm, long exposure.** The most restrictive case is a small source, one whose apparent angular size seen from the eye is $\\alpha\\le1.5$ mrad (§C4c subsection 1).\n  - For a small source, Table 3 of the standard gives a long-exposure limit of $3.9\\times10^{-4}C_4C_7$ W at 905 nm, where $C_7$ is a further correction factor that equals 1 at this wavelength. That is $3.9\\times10^{-4}\\times2.570=1.002\\times10^{-3}$ W, about **1.00 mW**, against **9.6 mW** at 1,550 nm (1 kW m⁻² over a 3.5 mm aperture; the standard's table entry is commonly quoted as 10 mW) [P/E].\n  - Per joule, 1,550 nm carries $1{,}550/905=1.713$ times as many photons as 905 nm, because a photon's energy is inversely proportional to its wavelength (§C4c subsection 1). Adjusted for photon energy, the ratio is therefore ≈16–17: $(9.6/1.00)\\times1.713=16.4$, or $(10/1.002)\\times1.713=17.1$ from the rounded 10 mW entry. That is the scale of Luminar's \"17×\" claim.\n  - **But the prescribed measurement apertures differ**: 7 mm for the retinal band (wavelengths the eye focuses onto the retina; 7 mm is roughly a dilated pupil), 3.5 mm for long 1,550 nm exposures. The ratio is therefore not a like-for-like comparison of transmitter output, real-scene photon flux, sensor-level range or compliance.\n- **Pulses: the edition matters.** The 2007 edition's single-pulse limit, $2\\times10^{-7}C_4$ J ($2\\times10^{-7}\\times2.570=0.514$ µJ at 905 nm and $2\\times10^{-7}\\times3.020=0.604$ µJ at 940 nm), was lowered in the 2014 edition to **198/233 nJ** per isolated small-source pulse, falling to **79/93 nJ** for long pulse trains. The per-pulse limit falls for trains because many pulses can enter the eye within the counting time $T_2=10$ s (§C4c subsection 1). The 1,550 nm single-pulse limit (7.85 mJ, ≈8 mJ in the standard's table) applies to pulses up to 0.35 s, so §C4c compares it with the 2014 near-IR values, not with the 2007 ones.",
   "why": "Use 2.570/3.020/1.175/17.5% as in §C4c; split the long bullet; photon ratio 16–17 with both 9.6 and 10 mW; edition bullet without the correction-note wording; drop duplicated 1.1749 arithmetic."
  },
  {
   "id": "04_C4_C4b_C4c-004",
   "finding": "report #8 (silicon absorption)",
   "old": "- **Silicon absorption:** the absorption coefficient is $\\alpha\\approx2.9\\times10^2$ cm⁻¹ at 905 nm ($1/\\alpha\\approx35$ µm) and $1.83\\times10^2$ cm⁻¹ at 940 nm ($1/\\alpha\\approx55$ µm) [I, Green 2008; the 905 nm value interpolated between the 900 and 910 nm entries]. Here $1/\\alpha$ is the absorption length, the depth over which the light falls to $1/e$, so a thin silicon detector lets most 9xx-nm photons pass unabsorbed: a 10 µm absorbing layer captures $1-e^{-\\alpha d}=25\\%$ of them at 905 nm and 17% at 940 nm in one pass, where $d$ is the layer thickness [E].",
   "new": "- **Silicon absorption:** silicon's absorption coefficient is $\\alpha_{\\rm Si}\\approx2.9\\times10^2$ cm⁻¹ at 905 nm ($1/\\alpha_{\\rm Si}\\approx35$ µm) and $1.83\\times10^2$ cm⁻¹ at 940 nm ($1/\\alpha_{\\rm Si}\\approx55$ µm) [I, Green 2008; the 905 nm value interpolated between the 900 and 910 nm entries]. Here $1/\\alpha_{\\rm Si}$ is the absorption length, the depth over which the light falls to $1/e$, so a thin silicon detector lets most 9xx-nm photons pass unabsorbed: a 10 µm absorbing layer captures $1-e^{-\\alpha_{\\rm Si}d}=1-e^{-0.29}=25\\%$ of them at 905 nm and $1-e^{-0.183}=17\\%$ at 940 nm in one pass, where $d=10^{-3}$ cm is the layer thickness [E].",
   "why": "alpha_Si distinguishes silicon absorption from the apparent source angle alpha; substitution shown for 25% and 17%."
  },
