   "id": "04_C4_C4b_C4c-008",
   "finding": "report polish (3278 untagged Falcon power, nested parentheses); follow-up #1 (partial); follow-up #5 (pointers)",
   "old": "- A 1,550 nm fibre MOPA (master-oscillator power amplifier: a seed laser amplified in an erbium-doped fibre amplifier (EDFA) that is pumped at 940 nm, with the output split into four beams) is expensive and hot. Falcon K drew 30 W and K2 20 W, against about 6–10 W for 9xx-nm units (Robin E1X, Hesai ATX).",
   "new": "- A 1,550 nm fibre MOPA is expensive and hot. A MOPA (master-oscillator power amplifier) amplifies a seed laser in an erbium-doped fibre amplifier (EDFA), which is pumped at 940 nm; here its output is split into four beams. Seyond's Falcon K drew 30 W [U], and its Falcon K2 is specified at 20 W [P, datasheet; §C3, §E9], against about 6–10 W for 9xx-nm units: the Seyond Robin E1X at 6 W [P] and the Hesai ATX at 8 W in its spec table or 10 W typical in its manual [P] (§C3b, §E9).",
   "why": "Unnest the MOPA definition; tag and point each power figure to where the report sources it; the 30 W has no source ([U], see skipped)."
  },
  {
   "id": "04_C4_C4b_C4c-009",
   "finding": "report #25 (item 4: ASTM numbers derived again)",
   "old": "- The reference is the ASTM G173-03 standard solar spectrum (global irradiance on a tilted surface). Its spectral irradiance $E_\\lambda$, the solar power per unit area per nm of wavelength, is, at the exact wavelength / averaged over ±10 nm: **0.82 / 0.69 W m⁻² nm⁻¹ at 905 nm**, **0.47 / 0.30 at 940 nm** (water-vapour band) and **0.27 / 0.27 at 1,550 nm** [P]. In photons, 940 nm has the lowest band-averaged background of the three. The photon flux per nm is $E_\\lambda/E_{\\rm ph}=E_\\lambda\\lambda/hc$, with $E_{\\rm ph}=hc/\\lambda$ the photon energy ($h$ Planck's constant, $c$ the speed of light), so the ±10 nm averages give $E_\\lambda\\lambda=0.69\\times905\\approx624$, $0.30\\times940=282$ and $0.27\\times1550\\approx419$ (in units proportional to photon flux): 940 nm is $624/282\\approx2.2$ times below 905 nm and $419/282\\approx1.5$ times below 1,550 nm. At the exact wavelengths ($0.47\\times940\\approx442$ against $0.27\\times1550\\approx419$) 1,550 nm is marginally lower, so the ranking rests on the band average. The advantage shrinks in dry air (§C4c).",
   "new": "- The reference is the ASTM G173-03 standard solar spectrum (global irradiance on a tilted surface). Its spectral irradiance $E_\\lambda$, the solar power per unit area per nm of wavelength, is, at the exact wavelength / averaged over ±10 nm: **0.82 / 0.69 W m⁻² nm⁻¹ at 905 nm**, **0.47 / 0.30 at 940 nm** (water-vapour band) and **0.27 / 0.27 at 1,550 nm** [P]. Converted to photons, which is what a detector counts, the band-averaged background at 940 nm is 0.46× that at 905 nm and 0.68× that at 1,550 nm (§C4c subsection 2). At the exact wavelengths 1,550 nm is marginally lower than 940 nm: $E_\\lambda\\lambda$, which is proportional to the photon flux, is $0.27\\times1{,}550\\approx419$ against $0.47\\times940\\approx442$, so the ranking rests on the band average. The advantage shrinks in dry air (§C4c subsection 2).",
   "why": "Quote the §C4c photon ratios instead of a second, rounder derivation; keep the exact-wavelength caveat."
  },
  {
