   "id": "04_C4_C4b_C4c-011",
   "finding": "report #1 (remaining part: 3285 sentence)",
   "old": "The 17.5% allowance (item 1) comes from one simplified single-pulse calculation; it is not a universal 940 nm system advantage.",
   "new": "The 17.5% higher emission limit (item 1) is the only intrinsic eye-safety gain of 940 nm; what it is worth at system level depends on those other factors.",
   "why": "Item 1 shows the 17.5% applies whichever Class 1 rule binds; this sentence contradicted it."
  },
  {
   "id": "04_C4_C4b_C4c-012",
   "finding": "report #25 (item 5: water derived again); report #8 (alpha for water)",
   "old": "**5. Liquid water penalises 1,550 nm.** The absorption coefficient of liquid water is about 10 cm⁻¹ at 1,550 nm vs about 0.07 cm⁻¹ at 905 nm, so a 1 mm water film absorbs about two-thirds of 1,550 nm light in one pass. By the Beer–Lambert law the absorbed fraction is $1-e^{-\\alpha d}$, where $\\alpha$ is the absorption coefficient (cm⁻¹) and $d$ the film thickness (cm). With $d=0.1$ cm this gives $1-e^{-1.0}\\approx63\\%$ for $\\alpha=10$ cm⁻¹ and $1-e^{-1.08}\\approx66\\%$ for 10.8 cm⁻¹ (the tabulated value used in §C4c), against $1-e^{-0.007}\\approx0.7\\%$ at 905 nm. That hurts wet windows and undercuts \"better in rain\" claims (§C4c).",
   "new": "**5. Liquid water penalises 1,550 nm.** The absorption coefficient of liquid water is 10.8–11.9 cm⁻¹ at 1,550 nm against about 0.07 cm⁻¹ at 905 nm. By the Beer–Lambert law, a 1 mm water film therefore absorbs about two-thirds of 1,550 nm light in one pass (it transmits 34%) but only 0.7% at 905 nm (§C4c subsection 5). That hurts wet windows and undercuts \"better in rain\" claims (§C4c subsection 5).",
   "why": "Result and pointer instead of a second Beer-Lambert derivation; removes the third meaning of alpha in §C4."
  },
  {
   "id": "04_C4_C4b_C4c-013",
   "finding": "report #17, #18",
   "old": "Étendue is the receiver's aperture area times the solid angle it accepts; optics cannot increase it, so a receiver that stares at the whole field at once must give up aperture. The mirror then needs only about 10 sweeps a second, against 1,000–3,000 for a single-beam 2-D MEMS mirror. The sweep rate is lines × frame rate ÷ parallel channels (§C4d), i.e. the number of mirror passes per second needed when each pass draws as many lines as there are channels working in parallel: 128 lines read by 128 parallel channels at 10 Hz need $128\\times10/128=10$ sweeps a second, while one beam drawing an assumed 100–300 lines at 10 Hz needs $(100\\ {\\rm to}\\ 300)\\times10=1{,}000$–$3{,}000$.",
   "new": "Étendue is the receiver's aperture area times the solid angle it accepts. Passive optics cannot increase it, and a detector of given area behind a lens of given f-number has a fixed étendue, so a receiver that stares at the whole field at once (a large solid angle) must accept a small aperture (§C0 subsection 3, §C6). The mirror then needs only about 10 sweeps a second, against 1,000–3,000 for a single-beam 2-D MEMS mirror. The required sweep rate is $S=N_{\\rm lines}f_{\\rm frame}/N_{\\rm par}$ (Eq. (69), §C4d), where $N_{\\rm lines}$ is the number of lines per frame, $f_{\\rm frame}$ the frame rate (Hz) and $N_{\\rm par}$ the number of lines drawn in parallel, one per channel. With 128 lines, 128 parallel channels and 10 Hz, $S=128\\times10/128=10$ sweeps/s; one beam drawing an assumed 100–300 lines at 10 Hz needs $S=(100\\ {\\rm to}\\ 300)\\times10/1=1{,}000$–$3{,}000$ sweeps/s.",
   "why": "Write the sweep rate with the symbols of Eq. (69) and shorten the 70-word sentence; add the fixed-étendue step."
  },
  {
