  {
   "id": "04_C4_C4b_C4c-029",
   "finding": "report polish (3386: four constraints but five subsections)",
   "old": "and answers it with numbers from four constraints: how much light the eye-safety standard lets the sensor emit, how much sunlight competes with the returning signal, what the detector material costs, and how much water on the window or the target absorbs.",
   "new": "and answers it with numbers from five constraints: how much light the eye-safety standard lets the sensor emit, how much sunlight competes with the returning signal, what InGaAs detectors cost, whether silicon-foundry germanium photodiodes can serve coherent receivers, and how much water on the window or the target absorbs (subsections 1–5; subsection 6 gives the verdict).",
   "why": "Match the five subsections."
  },
  {
   "id": "04_C4_C4b_C4c-030",
   "finding": "report #3 (remaining part: 3388 definition); consistency flag (emitter type)",
   "old": "\"905 nm\" here means the 905–910 nm band of multi-junction edge-emitting lasers (EELs, which emit from the cleaved edge of the chip). 940 nm is the band of vertical-cavity surface-emitting lasers (VCSELs, which emit from the chip surface and can be made as dense 1-D or 2-D arrays).",
   "new": "\"905 nm\" here means the 905–910 nm band, served by multi-junction edge-emitting lasers (EELs, which emit from the cleaved edge of the chip) and also by arrays of vertical-cavity surface-emitting lasers (VCSELs, which emit from the chip surface and can be made as dense 1-D or 2-D arrays), as in the Hesai AT128/ATX (§C3). 940 nm is the band of VCSELs.",
   "why": "§C3 lists Hesai AT/ATX as 905 nm VCSEL + SiPM, so 905 nm is not EEL-only."
  },
