   "id": "04_C4_C4b_C4c-041",
   "finding": "report #14 (3457)",
   "old": "- **Stationary beam.** Over the $T_2=10$ s time base a pulse repetition frequency $f_p$ (in Hz) gives",
   "new": "- **Stationary beam.** Over the counting time $T_2=10$ s, a pulse repetition frequency $f_p$ (the PRF, in Hz) gives",
   "why": "T_2 is the counting time; 'time base' is the classification's exposure duration."
  },
  {
   "id": "04_C4_C4b_C4c-042",
   "finding": "report #34",
   "old": "  - At 500 kHz the pulses are 2 µs apart, so pairs fall within one $T_i=5$ µs and count as one pulse carrying the energy of both (two pulses per group assumed). With $C_5$ at its 0.4 floor each pair may carry 79 nJ, i.e. ≈40 nJ per pulse; counting the average 2.5 pulses per $T_i$ instead would give ≈32 nJ.",
   "new": "  - At 500 kHz the pulses are 2 µs apart. Along a line they land $209\\ {\\rm mm}/(5\\times10^{5}\\ {\\rm s^{-1}}/1{,}280\\ {\\rm lines\\,s^{-1}})=0.54$ mm apart at 100 mm (a 120° line is $2.094\\ {\\rm rad}\\times100\\ {\\rm mm}=209$ mm long, and 128 lines at 10 Hz make 1,280 lines a second), far closer than the 10 mm width of the patch, so consecutive pulses both reach the aperture. Pairs therefore fall within one $T_i=5$ µs and count as one pulse carrying the energy of both (two pulses per group assumed). In $T_2$ the aperture receives five times the 100 kHz count, $5\\times1.09\\times10^{4}=5.5\\times10^{4}$ pulses, i.e. $2.7\\times10^{4}$ pairs, and Eq. (66) gives $(2.7\\times10^{4}/600)^{-1/4}=0.39$, below the floor, so $C_5=0.4$. Each pair may then carry $0.4\\times198=79$ nJ, i.e. ≈40 nJ per pulse; counting the average 2.5 pulses per $T_i$ instead would give ≈32 nJ.",
   "why": "Show why consecutive pulses both reach the aperture and the pulse count that puts C5 at its floor."
  },
  {
   "id": "04_C4_C4b_C4c-043",
   "finding": "report #33",
   "old": "  - The ratios are $91\\ {\\rm µJ}/96\\ {\\rm nJ}\\approx950$ and $18\\ {\\rm µJ}/40\\ {\\rm nJ}\\approx460$.",
   "new": "  - The ratios are $91\\ {\\rm µJ}/96\\ {\\rm nJ}\\approx950$ and $18.3\\ {\\rm µJ}/39.6\\ {\\rm nJ}\\approx460$, using the unrounded values $9.14\\ {\\rm W}/500\\ {\\rm kHz}=18.3$ µJ and $0.4\\times198/2=39.6$ nJ.",
   "why": "18/40 as written is 450; the unrounded inputs give 460."
  },
  {
   "id": "04_C4_C4b_C4c-044",
   "finding": "report #28; consistency flag (shots per point)",
   "old": "- **905/940 nm ToF is capped at ≈10–100 nJ per pulse** (more for scanned beams, within the $C_5$ floor). That is why lidars built on silicon single-photon avalanche diodes (SPADs, which register individual photons) fire ≈10 low-energy shots per point at high PRF and histogram them, i.e. accumulate the arrival times of many shots and take the peak as the echo (§C6).",
   "new": "- **905/940 nm ToF is capped at ≈10–100 nJ per pulse**: ≈10 nJ for a stationary beam at 100 kHz or more, ≈40–96 nJ for the scanned raster of the table, and never more than the 198/233 nJ single-pulse limit. That is why lidars built on silicon single-photon avalanche diodes (SPADs, which register individual photons) fire several low-energy shots per point at high PRF and histogram them, i.e. accumulate the arrival times of many shots and take the peak as the echo. A 300 m design has time for at most ≈10 shots per point (§C6); shorter-range designs can use more (§C0 subsection 10.8).",
   "why": "The scanned raster gives 40-96 nJ, inside 10-100 nJ; give the basis of '≈10 shots per point'."
  },
  {
   "id": "04_C4_C4b_C4c-045",
   "finding": "report #6",
   "old": "Eye safety is therefore not the binding limit on FMCW point rate; heat and wall-plug efficiency (optical output power per unit of electrical input power) are (§C7a). The §C7a model's FMCW needs 3.0 W at 10 Mpts/s and 7.6 W at 25 Mpts/s at 200 m; at 300 m and 25 Mpts/s its 11 W would exceed the allowance for this field (§C7b subsection 2(i)).",
   "new": "At 200 m, eye safety therefore does not limit FMCW point rate up to 25 Mpts/s: the §C7a model's FMCW needs 3.0 W at 10 Mpts/s and 7.6 W at 25 Mpts/s, within the 9.1 W allowance, and heat and wall-plug efficiency (optical output power per unit of electrical input power) bind first (§C7a). At 300 m and 25 Mpts/s, however, the model's 11 W would exceed the allowance for this field (§C7b subsection 2(i)).",
   "why": "The flat claim contradicted the next sentence's 11 W at 300 m; numbers unchanged."
  },
  {
   "id": "04_C4_C4b_C4c-046",
   "finding": "report #35 (ratios)",
   "old": "These are ratios of the photon-flux column: $1.44/3.16\\approx0.46$, $1.44/2.10\\approx0.68$ and $2.10/3.16\\approx0.67$.",
   "new": "These are ratios of the unrounded photon fluxes behind the photon-flux column: 940/905 ≈ 0.46, 940/1,550 ≈ 0.68 and 1,550/905 ≈ 0.67. The rounded entries shown give $1.44/3.16=0.46$, $1.44/2.10=0.69$ and $2.10/3.16=0.66$.",
   "why": "As displayed, 1.44/2.10 = 0.69 and 2.10/3.16 = 0.66; the stated ratios come from unrounded values (archived astm_g173_lidar_bands.py)."
  },
