   "id": "04_C4_C4b_C4c-051",
   "finding": "report #12 (derivation)",
   "old": "The 905 nm row uses $3.16\\times10^{18}$, a 20 nm filter and 0.24 ($1.6\\times10^{9}$); the 1,550 nm row uses $2.1\\times10^{18}$, 10 nm and 0.30 ($6.8\\times10^{8}$).",
   "new": "The 905 nm rows use $3.16\\times10^{18}$ and 0.24, with a 20 nm filter ($1.6\\times10^{9}$) or a 40 nm filter ($3.2\\times10^{9}$, taking the same ±10 nm average irradiance); the 1,550 nm row uses $2.1\\times10^{18}$, 10 nm and 0.30 ($6.8\\times10^{8}$). The 20 nm row assumes a temperature-stabilised source or a 905 nm VCSEL; an edge-emitting laser needs ≈40 nm over −40 to +105 °C (§C4 item 4), which doubles the count.",
