  {
   "id": "04_C4_C4b_C4c-056",
   "finding": "report #19, #39; consistency flag (solar background for the same case)",
   "old": "A 1,550 nm pixel receives ≈7×10⁸ background counts/s in bright sun (§2 table), about **700× the ≈10⁶ counts/s an InGaAs SPAD can sustain** with the hold-off its afterpulsing requires [E: $6.8\\times10^{8}/10^{6}\\approx700$]. A silicon 3×3 macro-pixel (nine SPADs whose counts are pooled into one ranging pixel) at 940 nm sits at ≈1× its ceiling [E: nine SPADs at 60 Mcps each sustain $5.4\\times10^{8}$ counts/s, and $5.7\\times10^{8}/(5.4\\times10^{8})=1.05$] (§C6(d)).",
   "new": "A 1,550 nm pixel receives ≈7×10⁸ background counts/s in bright sun (subsection 2 table above), about **700× the ≈10⁶ counts/s an InGaAs SPAD can sustain** with a 1 µs hold-off, which still leaves ≈8% afterpulsing [E: $6.8\\times10^{8}/10^{6}\\approx700$]. The 10 µs hold-off needed for <3% afterpulsing lowers the ceiling to ≈10⁵ counts/s, ≈7,000× below the sunlight rate [E: $6.8\\times10^{8}/10^{5}\\approx7{,}000$]. A silicon 3×3 macro-pixel (nine SPADs whose counts are pooled into one ranging pixel) at 940 nm sits at ≈1.0–1.35× its ceiling [E: nine SPADs at 60 Mcps each sustain $5.4\\times10^{8}$ counts/s; $5.7\\times10^{8}/(5.4\\times10^{8})=1.05$ with this subsection's band-averaged irradiance, and $7.31\\times10^{8}/(5.4\\times10^{8})=1.35$ with the higher irradiance of §C6(d)].",
   "why": "'§2' read as a report section; 700x assumes a 1 us hold-off; give both background conventions for the 940 nm case."
  },
  {
   "id": "04_C4_C4b_C4c-057",
   "finding": "report #29",
   "old": "| Platform / work | Responsivity at 1,550 nm | Dark current | Bandwidth |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| AIM (APSUNY process design kit, PDK), vertical PIN | >1 A/W at −1 V | — | ≈50 GHz |\n| imec, 1.8 × 15 µm² | 0.9 A/W | ≈15 nA at −1 V | >50 GHz |\n| GlobalFoundries (O-band), OFC 2025 | 0.95 A/W | <30 nA | ≈60 GHz |\n| Fudan / Zhangjiang Lab / Innolight, ultra-thin Ge | >0.8 A/W (C-band) | <20 nA | up to 100 GHz |\n| FMCW receiver engine (Moore and More, 2025) | 1.01–1.09 A/W over 18 PDs | 3.6 nA | saturation 6.3 mA |",
   "new": "| Platform / work | Responsivity (at 1,550 nm unless stated) | Dark current | Bandwidth | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| AIM Photonics (American Institute for Manufacturing Integrated Photonics), APSUNY process design kit (PDK, from Analog Photonics and SUNY Polytechnic Institute), vertical PIN | >1 A/W at −1 V | — | ≈50 GHz | — |\n| imec, 1.8 × 15 µm² | 0.9 A/W | ≈15 nA at −1 V | >50 GHz | — |\n| GlobalFoundries, OFC 2025 | 0.95 A/W (O-band) | <30 nA | ≈60 GHz | — |\n| Fudan / Zhangjiang Lab / Innolight, ultra-thin Ge | >0.8 A/W (C-band) | <20 nA | up to 100 GHz | — |\n| FMCW receiver engine (Moore and More, 2025) | 1.01–1.09 A/W over 18 PDs (photodiodes) | 3.6 nA | — | Saturation photocurrent 6.3 mA |",
   "why": "O-band device under a 1,550 nm column; saturation current sat in the bandwidth column; expand AIM, APSUNY and PDs."
  },
  {
   "id": "04_C4_C4b_C4c-058",
   "finding": "report polish (3574: 2qI called a noise power); consistency flag (i_n symbol)",
   "old": "equivalently, its noise power per hertz is $2qI$.",
   "new": "equivalently, its mean-square noise current per hertz is $2qI$ (A² Hz⁻¹). Here $i_n$ is a density, not the band-integrated mean square $\\langle i_n^2\\rangle$ of Eq. (26) (§C0 subsection 6).",
   "why": "2qI is a current spectral density, not a power; distinguish i_n from Eq. (26)."
  },
  {
   "id": "04_C4_C4b_C4c-059",
   "finding": "report #22",
   "old": "dark current of 15.8 µA per diode (50 nA at 25 °C, 0.5 eV activation energy), Eq. (68) gives",
   "new": "dark current of 15.8 µA per diode (50 nA at 25 °C, 0.5 eV activation energy) [E: $I_d(T)=I_d(T_0)\\,(T/T_0)^3\\,e^{-(E_a/k_B)(1/T-1/T_0)}$, with $I_d(T_0)=50$ nA at $T_0=298.15$ K, activation energy $E_a=0.5$ eV and Boltzmann's constant $k_B=8.617\\times10^{-5}$ eV/K; at $T=398.15$ K, $(T/T_0)^3=2.38$ and $e^{5{,}803\\ {\\rm K}\\times(1/298.15-1/398.15)\\ {\\rm K^{-1}}}=e^{4.89}=133$, so $I_d\\approx50\\ {\\rm nA}\\times2.38\\times133=15.8$ µA], Eq. (68) gives",
   "why": "Show the scaling law, inputs and substitution behind 15.8 µA (law from the figure caption)."
  },
  {
   "id": "04_C4_C4b_C4c-060",
   "finding": "report #30",
   "old": "and note that telecom coherent receivers \"typically require ~10–100× more LO power\".",
   "new": "and note that telecom coherent receivers \"typically require ~10–100× more LO power\". At 0.9 A/W, 5 µW gives 4.5 µA of LO photocurrent, so parity at that current implies an amplifier input noise of $\\sqrt{2qI_{\\rm LO}}=\\sqrt{2\\times1.602\\times10^{-19}\\times4.5\\times10^{-6}}\\approx1.2$ pA Hz⁻¹ᐟ², about 2.5 times quieter than the 3 pA Hz⁻¹ᐟ² assumed above [E]. The 0.3–1 mW used above therefore buys margin with a noisier amplifier; it is not a physical minimum.",
   "why": "Explain the gap between 5 µW and the 28 µA / 0.3-1 mW derived above."
  },
  {
   "id": "04_C4_C4b_C4c-061",
   "finding": "report #40",
   "old": "Germanium absorbs 1,550 nm light strongly only above the photon energy of its *direct band gap*, the \"direct edge\".",
   "new": "Germanium absorbs strongly only photons whose energy exceeds its *direct band gap*, the \"direct edge\"; in Ge-on-Si that edge lies just longward of 1,550 nm at room temperature.",
   "why": "The sentence mixed wavelength and photon energy."
  },
  {
   "id": "04_C4_C4b_C4c-062",
   "finding": "report polish (3598: 0.85 vs 0.86 nm/K; 1,600-1,625 vs 1,590 nm)",
   "old": "- Tensile-strained Ge-on-Si has its direct edge near 1,600–1,625 nm at room temperature. The edge moves to shorter wavelengths as temperature falls: ≈0.54 nm/K for the SPAD cut-off in Vines et al., ≈0.85 nm/K for the direct gap in the model above (at 0.2% strain the companion table puts the edge at 1,590 nm at 25 °C and 1,534 nm at −40 °C, $(1{,}590-1{,}534)\\ {\\rm nm}/65\\ {\\rm K}=0.86$ nm/K).",
